Canonical Source Boundary
===============================================================================

Status: S1 contract.

This document defines the source ownership boundary for the syntax
reimplementation after R1-R6. `docs/design/specification.mec` is the sole
language authority. The current Nom entry point is inventoried only so the
atomic cutover can remove every caller.

The authoritative inventories are:

- `grammar-audit/source-parser-consumers.tsv`: all 29 production calls grouped
  into 27 caller contracts, with current behavior and final canonical target;
- `grammar-audit/source-parser-consumer-fixtures.tsv`: whole-document canonical
  expectations shared by those caller contracts;
- `grammar-audit/phase-2i-recursive-core.tsv`: the exact 80-rule component; and
- `grammar-audit/phase-2i-syntax-schema.tsv`: the rule-level canonical tree
  contract.

1. Ownership
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The syntax crate owns UTF-8 source revisions, scanning, lossless trees, parser
recovery, structural diagnostics, strict structural validation, typed red-tree
views, and incremental identity.

Source tools consume canonical syntax directly:

```text
formatter / HTML renderer   DocumentSyntax plus lossless tokens
source index / resolver     typed imports, exports, contexts, scopes, paths
CLI source classification   typed document and Mech item structure
editor / interactive input  SyntaxSnapshot, revisions, diagnostics
```

The engine owns source semantics. Its canonical source frontend consumes a
strictly valid typed document and constructs the semantic forms used by
`SourceProgram`, `ProgramArtifact`, Type System v1, and memory planning.

The runtime owns activation, state, scheduling, resources, effects, and host
interfaces. It does not interpret concrete syntax.

2. Prohibited boundaries
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Canonical syntax, validation, and source-semantic modules do not call the Nom
parser or compare their output to it. The syntax crate does not depend on the
engine or runtime and does not construct compiler IR, `SourceProgram`,
`ProgramArtifact`, `Value`, memory plans, or runtime cells.

There is no canonical-to-`mech_core::Program` lowering tree, parser selector,
fallback route, or compatibility adapter. The product cutover replaces the
inventoried current callers and deletes the old source path in one milestone.

3. Strict validation
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Parsing is total and lossless. Strict validation is the gate for consumers that
require a complete source program. A snapshot fails strict validation when it
contains any of:

- parser diagnostics with error severity;
- an `ERROR` node or token;
- a `MISSING` node or token;
- a required typed child or token that is absent;
- token text invalid for its canonical syntax kind; or
- a parser resource-limit diagnostic that leaves the requested root incomplete.

Validation diagnostics use these stable fields:

```text
code       validation/<stable-condition>
phase      SyntaxValidation
severity   Error
rule       canonical RuleId when one owns the condition
context    internal ParserContextId when recovery owns the condition
primary    structural anchor when possible, absolute revision range otherwise
expected   canonical syntax kinds or terminals when applicable
found      observed canonical kind or token when applicable
recovery   ResourceLimit for exhausted parser resources
tags       stable diagnostic classification flags when applicable
```

English messages are presentation. Codes, phases, rules, anchors, expected and
found syntax, and tags are the test contract.

Validation never constructs partial semantic values. Consumers that can work
with incomplete input use the snapshot and typed optional accessors directly;
they do not invoke strict source semantics.

4. Current caller removal census
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`source-parser-consumers.tsv` records current call counts at function granularity
and assigns every caller a final boundary. Caller symbols include enclosing
modules and implementation or trait headers, so identically named methods do
not share a removal contract. Identical qualified paths in separate conditional
items also include their physical body offset. The census uses Rust syntax nodes
for conditional exclusion, including match arms, inner attributes and nested
`cfg_attr`. An exclusion requires proof that the node is unavailable without
tests; unknown feature and target predicates remain production possibilities.
Cargo's locked metadata and declared production dependencies supply local library,
binary and build-script targets. The initial graph starts at workspace packages and follows
non-development edges, including external intermediaries. Packages reached only
through development dependencies supply no production source root.
Discovery follows optional dependencies across separate workspaces, including
eligible local patches, until no packages remain to visit.
Cargo supplies package identities and version eligibility; discovery changes no
repository manifests or lockfiles.
Rust module declarations supply external files and `#[path]` module identities;
each file retains the compiling package's Cargo dependency names. Physical call
offsets are unioned across those namespaces, counting shared calls exactly once.
A `tests.rs` filename or `tests` directory never proves a test-only module.

The reference scan retains macro bodies and invocations, normalizes raw Rust
identifiers and resolves relative `self`/`super` imports against declaring
modules. It recognizes Cargo-renamed dependencies and guards both `extern crate
self` and standalone `use self` aliases. Opaque relative imports in macros are
rejected inside parser-exporting crates. Public and restricted crate-wide
re-exports and unrenamed parser-module re-exports receive no nested exemption.
Allowed parser-module imports cannot hide subsequent function aliases. Conditional
bindings in one lexical scope retain every possible retiring-parser reference.
Only the existing top-level root declarations are exempt; `document::parser`
remains distinct. Tests reject unlisted retiring
parser calls, aliases, alternate source entry points such as `parse_mech`, and
changes to the frozen 27 caller contracts and 29 calls. Existing declarations
are removal evidence, not permission for additional entry points. Macro token
streams are scanned conservatively; the census does not execute macro expansion.

The census describes current code only. Its current result-use and failure
columns do not grant authority to old AST shapes or behavior. The canonical
target and cutover-action columns define the intended replacement.

The target classes are:

```text
syntax-snapshot                 retain source revision and diagnostics
typed-document                  format, render, or inspect canonical structure
typed-source-index              derive imports, exports, contexts, and scopes
syntax-session                  append, edit, reset, and reuse source revisions
engine-source-semantics         compile typed source into canonical artifacts
engine-config-semantics         evaluate the canonical configuration subset
program-artifact                publish the canonical executable artifact
```

Composite targets join these responsibilities without introducing an
intermediate parser AST.

5. Canonical fixtures
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The S1 fixtures cover empty input, executable inline source, a mixed document,
source indexing, interactive mutation, compiler structures, configuration,
WASM document structure, and malformed input. Their accept/reject expectation
comes from the canonical specification.

S1 freezes their source and intended root. Later parser, recovery, typed-view,
semantic, and product-cutover milestones add canonical snapshots and consumer
assertions to the same fixtures. S1 deliberately does not execute the Nom
parser to generate or approve expected results.

6. Cutover invariant
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Before S8, the current product path is frozen for removal while the canonical
document root is completed. It receives no new behavior. New production work
uses canonical modules and cannot add an unlisted old-parser call.

S8 is complete only when the removal census reaches zero, source tools consume
typed canonical syntax, execution enters through engine source semantics, and
Nom plus `document/lower/legacy` are absent from production dependencies.


7. S4 semantic ownership and artifact completion
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The canonical source producer and resident providers share maintained operation
contracts from mech-core. Producer feature selection does not change variadic
construction, shape, alias, or change-detection policies. A provider-independent
contract describes semantics; availability of a resident implementation remains
an activation concern.

An undeclared source input may acquire a concrete schema from expression-local
peer constraints before a SourceProgram is finalized. Unannotated local pattern
projections from unresolved inputs can likewise acquire constrained element kinds;
their unresolved generator retains the existing source/bind artifact boundary.
An explicitly Dynamic
input is a boxed runtime value and cannot escape through a fabricated conversion:
Type System v1 has no cast from Dynamic to a concrete scalar or aggregate.
Optional construction preserves existing option layers and live dependencies.
A present Dynamic option boxes the original typed snapshot, including its schema
and shape, through the canonical ValueDraft constructor.

Constant-endpoint ranges activate and produce their specified values. Ranges
with live input or state endpoints retain the existing resident completion gate:
fixed arenas cannot support changing range cardinality. A correct peer-inferred
SourceProgram and serializable artifact do not claim that runtime capability;
activation rejects live ranges even when initial shape facts are supplied.

Selection preserves the source element kind and sequential subscript order.
Record and table fields use canonical Id selectors. Boolean selector populations
are bounded turn dimensions; activation supplies their resolved cardinality.
Existing resident arenas remain fixed for an activation, and a later mask with a
different selected cardinality must fail before publication. Set construction
retains the canonical duplicate-key rejection policy.

SourceProgram input names and source anchors remain canonical source identities.
At compile_artifact, every external input name uses the existing hexadecimal
interface-transport mechanism under the mech-source-input- namespace. The public
artifact_input_name, encode_source_input_name, and decode_source_input_name
accessors expose the injective, reversible transport mapping. This permits paths
and Unicode names without changing source identity or artifact ABI. Transporting
@ctx/path as an input does not implement context or capability resolution.

Source-only constructs without an executable operation contract retain an
explicit artifact completion boundary. Publishing an intermediate semantic graph
is not evidence that an artifact activates. The S4 regressions distinguish these
boundaries from supported selection, construction, and matrix operations whose
actual output values and changing input turns are tested. No production parser
caller changes here; those remain S8 work.
